  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure will bring continuing tranquil \nconditions through mid week. \n\nHigh pressure settling in over the area will bring dry and tranquil \nweather for the next few days. For today, expect a sunny sky \nwith light winds and low dew points making it feel quite \ncomfortable. Generally expect highs in the upper 70s to low 80s \nexcept low to mid 70s over the Pocono Plateau. A cool night will\nfollow for tonight with lows in much of the area getting down \ninto the 50s due to the light winds and mainly clear skies. \nRight along the coast, the Delaware Bay, and the Philly metro \narea it will be a bit warmer with lows generally in the low 60s.\nMore pleasant weather will follow for Tuesday with the high \npressure center right over the area. Expect continuing sunshine \nwith low humidity and slightly warmer temperatures compared to \nMonday. \n\nBy Wednesday, the center of high pressure will be shifting offshore \nleading to a return flow from the SW setting up. This will result in \na continuing warming trend with conditions also turning a bit more \nhumid as dew points increase. However it will be partly to mostly \nsunny with any showers/storms staying well north of the area through \nthe day. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will cross the area Thursday bringing \nthe potential for a round of showers and storms.\n\nWednesday night into Thursday, as high pressure continues to \nmove offshore, a southerly flow will continue to bring in warmer\nand more humid air. It is not entirely out of the question that\nas the ridge begins to break down some shower and storm chances\ncould ultimately evolve across portions of the area Wednesday \nnight over the Poconos into NW NJ where we currently have 15 to \n30% POPs. However the better chances for showers/storms looks to\narrive Thursday afternoon into the evening as a cold front \nmoves through north to south. That said, even for this time \nforecast confidence is relatively low as some forecast guidance \ncontinues to indicate a drier frontal passage. POPs for this \nperiod are generally in the 40-60% range. Currently, the threat \nfor severe storms or flash flooding concerns appears relatively \nlow.\n\nFollowing the frontal passage, high pressure building back in should \nbring a period of generally fair weather and seasonable temperatures \nfor Friday through next weekend.",
